---

See [Android platform hooks]($link.page('docs/android-extensions')) for the Android details, and
[App extensions]($link.page('docs/extensions')) for your own native code called from Zig.

A platform hook is the app's own code that the platform's own app, window
and web view events reach: a window or web view was created, an Activity
started, the page asked for a file chooser, the system opened a URL. A hook
reacts (configures the web view, keeps a reference, logs) or takes an event
over (returns "handled", and Oriel's default handling is skipped). Oriel
keeps its own clients, delegates and bridge; hooks sit next to them.

Platform hooks and [app extensions]($link.page('docs/extensions')) are different tools:

| | Platform hooks | App extensions |
|---|---|---|
| Who calls | The platform (through Oriel), when its events happen | Your Zig code, by name (`oriel.extensions.get("name").call(...)`); the page through what Zig exposes |
| What for | React to or take over the app's, windows' and web views' own events | Your native code as a service: a camera SDK, an on-device model, a vendor API |
| Declared with | `.hooks` (per platform) | `AppOptions.extensions` |

One class may be both: an Android class can implement `OrielAndroidHooks`
and `OrielAppExtension`.

# [Per platform]($heading.id('per-platform'))

Hooks are declared with `.hooks` on every platform, in the platform's own
language:

| Platform | Language | Declared with | Interface |
|---|---|---|---|
| Android | Kotlin or Java | `.android.hooks` in `build.zig` (`.android.extensions` is its deprecated name) | `OrielAndroidHooks` (`OrielAndroidExtension`): Activity and WebView lifecycle, file chooser, activity and permission results ([details]($link.page('docs/android-extensions'))) |
| iOS | Objective-C or Swift | `.ios.hooks` in `build.zig` | `@protocol OrielPlatformHooks` in `oriel_hooks.h` ([below]($link.ref('ios-and-macos'))) |
| macOS | Objective-C or Swift | `.macos.hooks` in `build.zig` | `@protocol OrielPlatformHooks` in `oriel_hooks.h` ([below]($link.ref('ios-and-macos'))) |
| Linux, Windows, macOS | Zig | `oriel.hooks.subscribe` at runtime | windows and web views with their native handles ([below]($link.ref('zig-oriel-hooks'))) |

macOS has both: Objective-C/Swift hooks for AppKit's app and window
callbacks, and the Zig hooks for code that already lives in Zig.

# [Zig: `oriel.hooks`]($heading.id('zig-oriel-hooks'))

The app's Zig code sees each window and web view Oriel creates, with its
native handle, and can configure it the platform's way (a WebKitGTK setting,
an NSWindow property, a WebView2 option) before the page loads. Linux, macOS
and Windows; `oriel.hooks.available` is false on Android and iOS, where
`subscribe` still succeeds and no handler is ever called (use the native
hooks there).

```zig
const oriel = @import("oriel");

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    // Before oriel.main, so the main window is seen too.
    if (oriel.hooks.available) try oriel.hooks.subscribe(.{
        .window_created = windowCreated,
        .webview_created = webViewCreated,
    });
    return oriel.main(init, api, config);
}

fn windowCreated(w: oriel.hooks.Window) void {
    std.log.info("window {s}: {s}", .{ w.label, @tagName(w.native) });
}

fn webViewCreated(v: oriel.hooks.WebView) void {
    switch (v.native) {
        // WebKitGTK's own API, through the handle.
        .webkitgtk => |view| if (comptime oriel.target.is_desktop_linux) {
            const settings = webkit_web_view_get_settings(view);
            webkit_settings_set_enable_smooth_scrolling(settings, 1);
        },
        .wkwebview, .webview2 => {},
    }
}

extern fn webkit_web_view_get_settings(view: *anyopaque) *anyopaque;
extern fn webkit_settings_set_enable_smooth_scrolling(settings: *anyopaque, enabled: c_int) void;
```

The showcase does this in `examples/showcase/src/platform_hooks.zig`.

## [API]($heading.id('api'))

```zig
pub const available: bool; // Linux, macOS, Windows

pub const Handlers = struct {
    window_created: ?*const fn (window: Window) void = null,
    window_destroyed: ?*const fn (window: Window) void = null,
    webview_created: ?*const fn (webview: WebView) void = null,
    webview_destroyed: ?*const fn (webview: WebView) void = null,
};

pub const max_subscribers = 16;
pub fn subscribe(handlers: Handlers) error{TooManySubscribers}!void;

pub const Window = struct { label: []const u8, native: NativeWindow };
pub const WebView = struct { label: []const u8, native: NativeWebView };

pub const NativeWindow = union(enum) {
    gtk: *anyopaque, // GtkWindow* (a GtkApplicationWindow), Linux
    appkit: *anyopaque, // NSWindow*, macOS
    win32: *anyopaque, // HWND, Windows
};

pub const NativeWebView = union(enum) {
    webkitgtk: *anyopaque, // WebKitWebView* (WebKitGTK 6.0), Linux
    wkwebview: *anyopaque, // WKWebView*, macOS
    webview2: struct {
        core: *anyopaque, // ICoreWebView2*
        controller: *anyopaque, // ICoreWebView2Controller*
    },
};
```

- **Subscribing.** `subscribe` takes a struct of optional handlers; set the
  ones you need. Up to 16 subscriptions; there is no unsubscribe. It may be
  called from any thread. Subscribe before `oriel.main` (or `App.run`) to
  see the main window: `App.Config.setup` runs after it opened, and windows
  that already exist are not replayed.
- **Threads and order.** Handlers run on the main thread, in subscription
  order. Per window: `window_created`, then `webview_created`; at the end
  `webview_destroyed`, then `window_destroyed`. Return quickly, and don't
  open or close windows from a handler.
- **When.** The created hooks run once the window holds its web view and
  Oriel has installed its bridge (the `window.oriel` script, the asset
  scheme handler, navigation and permission policy), before the first
  navigation and before the window is first shown.
  The destroyed hooks run when Oriel destroys the window (closed, or the
  app quitting on Linux and macOS; on Windows, windows still open at quit go
  with the process, without the hooks), while both are still alive.
- **Lifetime.** `label` is borrowed: copy it to keep it. A native handle is
  valid from its created hook until its destroyed hook returns. Oriel owns
  it: don't destroy, close or release it (retain or `AddRef` it to keep an
  object longer, and drop that reference by the destroyed hook).
- **What to leave alone.** Oriel's handlers on the web view (WebKitGTK's
  `decide-policy` and `permission-request`, the WKWebView navigation and UI
  delegates, WebView2's event handlers), its user scripts and message
  handlers, and the page it loads. Settings, extra signal handlers, extra
  user scripts of your own and window properties are fine.
- **Native renderer windows** (`-Dnative_ui`) have no web view: only the
  window hooks run.

# [Android]($heading.id('android'))

`.android.hooks` registers Kotlin or Java classes that implement
`OrielAndroidHooks` (a Kotlin `typealias` of `OrielAndroidExtension`, the
interface's name before Oriel 0.11, which Java classes implement):

```zig
.android = .{
    .sources = &.{b.path("src/android/MyHooks.kt")},
    .hooks = &.{"dev.example.MyHooks"},
},
```

```kotlin
class MyHooks : OrielAndroidHooks {
    override fun onWebViewCreated(view: WebView, windowLabel: String) {
        if (windowLabel == "main") view.settings.textZoom = 110
    }
}
```

The interface has Activity lifecycle callbacks, WebView created / attached /
detached / destroyed (after Oriel installs its clients and bridge, before
navigation), `onShowFileChooser` (return `true` to own the request), and
activity and permission results with a request-code range per class.
`.android.extensions`, the deprecated name, still works: its classes are
registered after the `.android.hooks` ones, and a class named in both is
registered once. [Android platform hooks]($link.page('docs/android-extensions')) has the
details: registration, lifetimes, request codes, file choosers, Maven SDKs
and vendor libraries.

# [iOS and macOS]($heading.id('ios-and-macos'))

Objective-C or Swift classes declared with `.ios.hooks` and `.macos.hooks`
adopt `@protocol OrielPlatformHooks` from `oriel_hooks.h`
(`src/platform/apple/include`, on the include path of the hook sources).
They take part in Oriel's app delegate, scene delegate (iOS), windows and
WebKit delegates without replacing them, with the same model as Android:
notifications, and interceptions that return `YES` to own a request.

## [Declare]($heading.id('apple-declare'))

```zig
_ = oriel.addApp(b, dep, .{
    // ...
    .ios = .{ .hooks = &.{
        .{
            .class = "AppHooks", // conforms to OrielPlatformHooks, plain -init
            .sources = &.{b.path("ios/AppHooks.m")}, // zig cc -fobjc-arc
            .frameworks = &.{"SafariServices"},
        },
        .{ .class = "PushHooks", .swift_sources = &.{b.path("apple/PushHooks.swift")} },
    } },
    .macos = .{ .hooks = &.{
        .{ .class = "AppHooks", .sources = &.{b.path("macos/AppHooks.m")} },
    } },
});
```

Each entry is `.class`, `.sources` (Objective-C, compiled with ARC),
`.swift_sources` (one `swiftc` object per hook) and `.frameworks` (SDK
frameworks to link). Up to 16 hooks per platform; an invalid or duplicate
class name, more than 16 hooks, or an invalid framework name is a build
error on any host. A class whose sources come from elsewhere (an app
extension's `.ios.sources`, say) can be listed without sources.

## [Write a hook]($heading.id('apple-write-a-hook'))

```objc
// ios/AppHooks.m
#import <SafariServices/SafariServices.h>
#import "oriel_hooks.h"

@interface AppHooks : NSObject <OrielPlatformHooks>
@property (nonatomic, strong) OrielHooksContext *context;
@end

@implementation AppHooks

- (void)registered:(OrielHooksContext *)context {
    self.context = context; // keep it: its properties are read live
}

- (void)webViewCreated:(WKWebView *)webView window:(NSString *)label {
    NSLog(@"web view for %@", label); // Oriel's delegates and bridge are in place
}

- (BOOL)webView:(WKWebView *)webView
    decidePolicyForNavigationAction:(WKNavigationAction *)action
                    decisionHandler:(void (^)(WKNavigationActionPolicy))decisionHandler {
    if (![action.request.URL.host isEqualToString:@"help.example.com"]) return NO; // Oriel decides
    decisionHandler(WKNavigationActionPolicyCancel); // exactly once
    UIViewController *front = self.context.presentingViewController;
    [front presentViewController:[[SFSafariViewController alloc] initWithURL:action.request.URL]
                        animated:YES completion:nil];
    return YES; // owned: no later hook, nor Oriel, sees it
}

@end
```

In Swift, the class needs `@objc(Name)` so Oriel finds it under the declared
name. The bridging header is `oriel_swift.h`, which imports both
`oriel_hooks.h` and `oriel_extension.h`:

```swift
// apple/PushHooks.swift
import Foundation
import UserNotifications

@objc(PushHooks)
final class PushHooks: NSObject, OrielPlatformHooks {
    func registered(_ context: OrielHooksContext) {
        UNUserNotificationCenter.current().requestAuthorization(options: [.alert]) { granted, _ in
            guard granted else { return }
            DispatchQueue.main.async { UIApplication.shared.registerForRemoteNotifications() }
        }
    }

    func didRegisterForRemoteNotifications(withDeviceToken token: Data) {
        NSLog("APNs token: %@", token.map { String(format: "%02x", $0) }.joined())
    }
}
```

## [The model]($heading.id('apple-the-model'))

- Oriel constructs each class once (`alloc`/`init`), in declaration order,
  at launch: on iOS in `application:didFinishLaunchingWithOptions:`, before
  the first scene; on macOS before the main window. The object lives as
  long as the process.
- Every method is optional and runs on the main thread. Oriel asks
  `respondsToSelector:` once per method, at construction.
- Notifications go to every hook implementing them, in declaration order.
- Interceptions return `BOOL`. `YES`: the hook owns the request and calls
  the handler it was given exactly once (now or later, on the main thread);
  later hooks and Oriel don't see it. `NO`: the next hook decides, then
  Oriel, as if there were no hooks. The first `YES` wins.
- A class listed both as a hook and as an [app extension]($link.page('docs/extensions'))
  (`.extensions[].ios`, or `.macos = .{ .objc = ... }`) is constructed once;
  both get the same object. List its sources in one place only.
- Don't throw Objective-C exceptions: nothing catches them.

## [Methods]($heading.id('apple-methods'))

| Method | Platform | Kind | When |
|---|---|---|---|
| `registered:(OrielHooksContext *)` | both | notify | right after `init` |
| `webViewConfiguration:(WKWebViewConfiguration *) window:(NSString *)` | both | notify | before the `WKWebView` is created; Oriel's scheme handler and bridge are already set (don't replace the `userContentController` or Oriel's handlers) |
| `webViewCreated:(WKWebView *) window:(NSString *)` | both | notify | after Oriel installs its delegates and bridge, before the first navigation (with `oriel.hooks`' `webview_created` on macOS) |
| `webViewDestroyed:(WKWebView *) window:(NSString *)` | both | notify | the window is being destroyed |
| `webView:decidePolicyForNavigationAction:decisionHandler:` | both | intercept | before Oriel's navigation policy |
| `webView:requestMediaCapturePermissionForOrigin:initiatedByFrame:type:decisionHandler:` | both | intercept | getUserMedia, before Oriel's rule |
| `openURL:(NSURL *)` | both | intercept | a URL of the app's schemes (not file URLs); `NO` leaves it to `oriel.deep_link` |
| `continueUserActivity:(NSUserActivity *)` | both | intercept | universal links, Handoff (iOS: `scene:continueUserActivity:` and a connecting scene's activities; macOS: `application:continueUserActivity:restorationHandler:`) |
| `didRegisterForRemoteNotificationsWithDeviceToken:(NSData *)` | both | notify | forwarded from Oriel's app delegate |
| `didFailToRegisterForRemoteNotificationsWithError:(NSError *)` | both | notify | forwarded from Oriel's app delegate |
| `applicationDidFinishLaunching:(UIApplication *) options:(NSDictionary *)` | iOS | notify | after `registered:`, before the main window and the app's `setup` |
| `sceneWillConnect:(UIScene *) options:(UISceneConnectionOptions *)` | iOS | notify | after Oriel shows its window in the scene, before the scene's URLs and activities |
| `sceneDidDisconnect:`, `sceneDidBecomeActive:`, `sceneWillResignActive:`, `sceneWillEnterForeground:`, `sceneDidEnterBackground:` | iOS | notify | the scene delegate's own events |
| `applicationDidFinishLaunching:(NSNotification *)` | macOS | notify | when the run loop starts: after the main window opened and `setup` ran |
| `windowCreated:(NSWindow *) label:(NSString *)` | macOS | notify | an Oriel window exists, before its page loads |
| `windowWillClose:(NSWindow *) label:(NSString *)` | macOS | notify | the window closes (not when it is only hidden) |
| `applicationShouldHandleReopen:hasVisibleWindows:` | macOS | intercept | a Dock click or relaunch; `YES` skips Oriel's (showing the main window, `on_second_instance`) |

`oriel_hooks.h` documents each method. Windows drawn by `-Dnative_ui` have
no web view: only their window methods are called.

`OrielHooksContext` (passed to `registered:`) has `name` (the class name)
and, read live, `presentingViewController` on iOS (the controller in front,
to present native UI from; nil before the first scene shows the main
window) or `keyWindow` on macOS (the key window, else Oriel's main window).

Allowing a navigation Oriel would block bypasses its navigation rules; the
bridge still checks every call's origin against the app's own.

## [Building]($heading.id('apple-building'))

- **Objective-C sources** compile with Zig's clang (`-fobjc-arc`) against
  the platform SDK. iOS builds need the iOS SDK anyway ([iOS]($link.page('docs/mobile'))). On
  macOS the SDK is `-Dmacos_sdk=<MacOSX.sdk>`, else `$SDKROOT` when it is a
  MacOSX SDK, else `xcrun --sdk macosx --show-sdk-path`: a Mac with Xcode
  or the Command Line Tools. Without one, a macOS build with hook sources
  is a build error naming what to install. `zig build check` never compiles
  hook sources, so it works without an SDK.
- **Swift sources** compile with `swiftc` (`$SWIFTC`, else Xcode's, else
  `swiftc` on `PATH`) for `arm64-apple-ios18.0` (or `-simulator`) or
  `arm64-apple-macos15.0` / `x86_64-apple-macos15.0`, one object per hook,
  linked with the OS's Swift runtime (/usr/lib/swift). iOS Swift can be
  built on Linux with xtool's SDK ([App extensions]($link.page('docs/extensions'))); macOS
  Swift needs a Mac.
- **Swift overlays link by themselves.** `swiftc` records the libraries a
  module needs (`import CoreML` → `libswiftCoreML`, `-framework CoreML`) as
  autolink entries, which Zig's linker ignores. So Oriel links the frameworks
  the Swift sources `import`, every `libswift*.tbd` of the SDK, and turns on
  `dead_strip_dylibs`: the executable loads only the libraries it uses, and
  every framework the app and Oriel link otherwise is kept (marked needed).
  Frameworks that only an Objective-C source uses still go in `.frameworks`.
- **Minimum versions with Swift.** iOS 18 and macOS 15 split the Swift
  Darwin overlay into new libraries (`libswift_errno`, …); Apple's linker
  records the old `libswiftDarwin` for older targets, Zig's doesn't. So an
  app with Swift hooks (or Swift app extensions) targets iOS 18 / macOS 15:
  the minimum is raised when the target names no version, and an explicit
  lower one (`-Dtarget=aarch64-ios.17.0`, `-Dtarget=aarch64-macos.14.0`)
  is a build error. Without Swift, Oriel's minimums stay (iOS 15,
  macOS 13).

The showcase declares `ShowcaseHooks` for both (Objective-C,
`examples/showcase/extensions/ios/` and `.../macos/`): it logs what it sees
(`showcase.hooks: ...`) and owns navigation to example.com (the App tab's
"Platform hooks" link): an in-app Safari sheet on iOS, the browser on
macOS. `-Dswift_example` adds `ShowcaseSwiftHooks`
(`extensions/apple/SwiftHooks.swift`), which imports CoreML, CoreGraphics
and Metal.
